        "id": "def-ip-gaussian-sequence-inverse-model",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Gaussian sequence inverse model",
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $X$ and $Y$ be real Hilbert spaces and let $K:X\\to Y$ be a compact linear operator of infinite rank, viewed as an inverse model with exact datum $Kx$. Here compact means that the image of every bounded subset has compact closure. Write $N(K)=\\{x\\in X:Kx=0\\}$ and $H_0=\\overline{\\operatorname{ran}K}$. Fix a positive singular system $(s_j,v_j,u_j)_{j\\in\\mathbb N}$ for $K$, where $\\mathbb N=\\{1,2,3,\\ldots\\}$, $s_1\\ge s_2\\ge\\cdots>0$, $s_j\\to0$, $Kv_j=s_ju_j$, $(v_j)$ is a complete orthonormal basis of $N(K)^\\perp$, and $(u_j)$ is a complete orthonormal basis of $H_0$. A complete orthonormal basis is an orthonormal family whose closed linear span is the whole indicated subspace. These are the compact SVD conventions; in particular, this model concerns minimum-norm representatives in $N(K)^\\perp$ and does not estimate a kernel component.\n\nFor a truth $x\\in N(K)^\\perp$, let $\\theta_j=\\langle x,v_j\\rangle_X$. The complete-basis coefficient map identifies $N(K)^\\perp$ bijectively and isometrically with $\\ell^2(\\mathbb N,\\mathbb R)$, so every $\\theta\\in\\ell^2(\\mathbb N,\\mathbb R)$ is a model parameter. For $\\epsilon>0$, take the functional white-noise experiment on the separable Hilbert space $H_0$ with observations $Y_x(g)=\\langle Kx,g\\rangle_Y+\\epsilon W(g)$, where $W$ is centered, has covariance $\\mathbb E[W(g)W(h)]=\\langle g,h\\rangle_Y$, and has independent standard-normal values on every finite orthonormal tuple. The countable complete basis makes $H_0$ separable. Define $\\xi_j=W(u_j)$ and $Z_j=Y_x(u_j)/s_j$. The sequence sample space is $\\mathbb R^{\\mathbb N}$ with the product Borel sigma-algebra, meaning the sigma-algebra generated by its coordinate projections. The coordinate law is the pushforward of the functional experiment under $y\\mapsto(y(u_j)/s_j)_{j\\in\\mathbb N}$; this map is measurable because each coordinate is a cylinder evaluation followed by scalar multiplication. The compact SVD expansion gives $\\langle Kx,u_j\\rangle_Y=s_j\\theta_j$, and the positive-noise white-noise coordinate theorem gives the independent standard-normal coordinates $\\xi_j$. Hence the normalized coordinates satisfy $Z_j=\\theta_j+\\epsilon s_j^{-1}\\xi_j,\\qquad j\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nFor the zero-noise boundary $\\epsilon=0$, define the sequence experiment separately by the point mass at $\\theta$, so $Z_j=\\theta_j$. This boundary is not an instance of the positive-noise functional experiment.",
