        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "Exact worst-case risk of the truncated estimator",
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice and let $\\epsilon\\ge0$. In the Gaussian sequence inverse model with positive singular numbers $(s_j)_{j\\in\\mathbb N}$, let $Q\\ge0$ and let $(a_j)_{j\\in\\mathbb N}$ be positive and nondecreasing. For each $\\theta\\in\\Theta_a(Q)$, define the squared sequence risk of the cutoff estimator at $D\\in\\mathbb N_0$ by\n$$R_\\theta(D):=\\mathbb E_\\theta\\!\\left[\\|\\widehat\\theta_D(Z)-\\theta\\|_{\\ell^2}^2\\right].$$\nThen its exact worst-case squared sequence risk is\n$$\\sup_{\\theta\\in\\Theta_a(Q)}R_\\theta(D)\n=\\frac{Q^2}{a_{D+1}^2}+\\epsilon^2\\sum_{j=1}^{D}s_j^{-2},$$\nwhere the sum is $0$ for $D=0$. The supremum is attained by the one-coordinate truth $\\theta^\\star=(Q/a_{D+1})e_{D+1}$, with $\\theta^\\star=0$ when $Q=0$.",
